\title{Sigma Protocols}
\author{dhsorens}
\date{2026-08-20}

\p{
    A \strong{sigma protocol} is a three-move [public-coin](dhsorens-0053) protocol in which the
    prover commits, the verifier sends one random challenge, and the prover responds. It is the
    smallest interesting interactive proof of knowledge, and almost every definition in this corpus
    was first written down for this case and then generalised. The name is Cramer's, from the shape
    of the message flow — down, across, down — and the canonical instance is Schnorr's
    identification protocol.
}

\p{
    The class matters for a historical reason as much as a mathematical one: Fiat and Shamir's 1986
    construction, which turned an interactive identification scheme into a signature by replacing
    the verifier's challenge with a hash, was defined for exactly these protocols. [The
    transform](dhsorens-004U) that carries their name has since been applied to protocols with
    twenty-odd rounds, but its original setting had one, and several of the results that hold
    comfortably here degrade badly there.
}

\p{
    Let #{R} be a binary relation, with #{(x,w) \in R} read as "#{w} is a witness for #{x}", and let
    #{L} be the set of #{x} admitting a witness.
}

\p{
    \strong{Definition.} A \em{sigma protocol} for #{R} consists of a finite challenge set #{C}, two
    prover algorithms #{P_1, P_2}, and a verifier predicate #{V}, run as follows. The prover draws
    randomness #{r}, computes the \em{commitment} #{a = P_1(x, w; r)} and sends it; the verifier
    draws #{c} uniformly from #{C} and sends it; the prover computes the \em{response}
    #{z = P_2(x, w, r, c)} and sends it; the verifier accepts exactly when #{V(x, a, c, z) = 1}.
    Three properties are required.
    \ul{
        \li{\strong{Completeness.} For every #{(x,w) \in R}, an honest execution accepts with
        probability #{1}.}
        \li{\strong{Special soundness.} There is a polynomial-time extractor which, given #{x} and
        two accepting transcripts #{(a, c, z)} and #{(a, c', z')} that share the commitment and
        satisfy #{c \neq c'}, outputs a #{w} with #{(x,w) \in R}.}
        \li{\strong{Special honest-verifier zero knowledge.} There is a polynomial-time simulator
        which, given #{x \in L} and a challenge #{c \in C}, outputs a pair #{(a,z)} such that
        #{(a,c,z)} is distributed exactly as an honest [transcript](dhsorens-0054) conditioned on
        that challenge.}
    }
}

\p{
    Schnorr's protocol is the instance to keep in mind. Work in a cyclic group #{\mathbb{G}} of
    prime order #{q} with generator #{g}, and let the relation pair a group element #{h} with a #{w}
    satisfying #{h = g^w}. The prover sends #{a = g^r} for uniform #{r}; the challenge is a uniform
    #{c \in \mathbb{Z}_q}; the response is #{z = r + cw \bmod q}; the verifier accepts when
    #{g^z = a h^c}. Each property is a line of algebra. Completeness is substitution. For special
    soundness, two accepting transcripts on the same #{a} give #{g^{z - z'} = h^{c - c'}}, and since
    #{c \neq c'} is invertible modulo the prime #{q}, the extractor returns
    #{w = (z - z')(c - c')^{-1}}. For simulation, given #{c} pick #{z} uniformly and set
    #{a = g^z h^{-c}}, which satisfies the verification equation by construction and is uniformly
    distributed exactly as an honest commitment is.
}

\p{
    Four features of this definition explain why it became the template. The soundness error is
    #{1/N}, where #{N} is the size of #{C}: a prover without a witness can commit to a value that
    works for one guessed challenge and cannot do better, so the challenge set alone sets the
    security of a single run. Special soundness is a property of the acceptance predicate rather
    than a probability bound, so it is verified by exhibiting the extraction algebra rather than by
    a probabilistic argument, which is why it is the workhorse hypothesis — [the general theory of
    it](dhsorens-004K) starts here. The zero-knowledge condition is stated for a simulator that is
    \em{given} the challenge, which looks like a technical weakening and is in fact precisely the
    interface a Fiat–Shamir simulator has in the [random oracle model](dhsorens-004V), where it
    fixes the challenge first and programs the oracle to return it. And the knowledge extractor
    works by rewinding: run the prover, save its state after #{a}, then continue it twice on
    different challenges. Rewinding is unobjectionable while the protocol is interactive, and is
    exactly what [stops being free](dhsorens-004J) once the prover is a single non-interactive
    algorithm.
}

\p{
    The generalisation to many rounds is #{(k_1, \dots, k_\mu)}-special soundness, where the
    extractor is handed a tree of accepting transcripts rather than a pair. That generalisation, and
    the coordinate-wise variant the lattice constructions need, is the subject of [special
    soundness](dhsorens-004K). The modern proof systems — [FRI](dhsorens-0037),
    [Spartan](dhsorens-003G), [WHIR](dhsorens-003C) — are not sigma protocols and cannot be analysed
    as though they were: they have many rounds, and for some of their components extraction does not
    go through a transcript tree at all but through [list decoding](dhsorens-002P). The reason to
    start here anyway is that the vocabulary was fixed in this setting, and a definition read in a
    modern paper is usually a generalisation of one of the three above.
}
